<Project>

  <!--
    Central Package Management: the single place a package version is declared
    (ADR-0026 section C, "Central Package Management is the single place that
    declares versions, which makes scanning and pinning straightforward").

    It lands with the first package rather than with the first project, and that
    was deliberate. `ManagePackageVersionsCentrally` over zero packages proves
    nothing at all: nothing can violate it, so nothing can demonstrate it works.
    With one package it is measurable, and it was measured on 2026-08-02:
    putting `Version="5.6.0"` back on the PackageReference fails the build with
    NU1008, and deleting the PackageVersion line below fails it with NU1010.
    Two different errors for two different mistakes, both exit 1.

    This file is also the answer to an item two documents have been carrying.
    ADR-0061's maintenance rule and `docs/DEPENDENCY-LICENSES.md` section 7 both
    say the durable completeness check is to reconcile their prose lists against
    the real dependency manifest "once code exists at M1", because two lists
    derived from prose can agree with each other while both omit the same thing.
    That manifest is this file. It is one row long, so the check is not worth
    automating yet, but it is no longer blocked, and it should be automated
    before the row count makes a manual read unreliable.

    A VERSION WRITTEN LIKE THE ONE BELOW IS A FLOOR, NOT A PIN, and the two are
    easy to confuse in exactly the file whose job is pinning. Read out of
    `obj/project.assets.json` after restore on 2026-08-02, this declaration
    produces the constraint `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers >= 5.6.0`
    and resolves to `5.6.0`, because NuGet takes the lowest version satisfying a
    direct constraint. The resolution is what a reader sees and the constraint
    is what is actually written, so a yanked or unlisted 5.6.0 would float this
    forward silently. Exact pinning is the bracket form, `[5.6.0]`.

    This is not a defect today and it is a decision owed before OpenIddict
    lands: ADR-0021 parameter A wants OpenIddict "pinned exactly through Central
    Package Management", and "exactly" is not what the plain form expresses.
    Whether every row here becomes bracketed, or only the engine's, is an open
    item and belongs in ADR-0021's own terms rather than being settled by
    whoever adds the next line to this file.

    The restore graph is currently ONE node deep, also read from the assets
    file: the analyzer brings no transitive packages at all. Worth knowing when
    the ADR-0026 licence-scan gate is wired, because a green scan over a
    one-node graph says almost nothing about the scanner.

    Licence, before adoption rather than after (ADR-0026, and section 7 of the
    licence record: "read the licence text of the thing that is actually
    distributed"). Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers 5.6.0 is MIT, read
    on 2026-08-02 in the package's own .nuspec fetched from the nuget.org flat
    container, which carries `<license type="expression">MIT</license>`. Two
    documents in this repository already asserted MIT for it in prose, which is
    blind spot 2 of the licence record and is not evidence. Recorded with its
    source in `docs/DEPENDENCY-LICENSES.md` section 3.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" Version="5.6.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
